  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Periods of rain and breezy conditions today as \nPost-Tropical Cyclone Isaias impacts the region\n\nSteady rain will continue today into the late afternoon. PW \nvalues will peak around 18z today in the 1.50-2.00\" range. Rain\nwill begin transitioning to a more convective rain shower regime\nthis evening. As the remnant sfc low from Post-Tropical Cyclone\nIsaias makes its closest approach this evening, it could bring \nenough elevated instability to increase thunderstorm potential \nfor areas south of Interstate 80. Highest chance for thunder \nwill be across the Lower Susq Valley. With a sfc inversion still\nin place at the time, severe weather is not expected. However, \nSPC does draw a MRGL risk for severe weather just south of the \nMason-Dixon line.\n\nTotal rainfall is expected to range from 1.50-2.50 inches for \nmost locations south and east of the I-80/I-99 corridor, with a\nroughly 30% chance of over 3 inches for locations along the \nsouthern tier, particularly if training/pivoting convective \nelements develop this evening. However, we continue to believe \nthere will not be any significant flooding. Rainfall rates will \nnot be particularly high for a majority of the event, and the \nrecent dry spell will also provide a cushion. However, localized\nminor flooding can not be ruled out in the south and east.\n\nExpect gusty winds in the tightening pressure gradient between \nthe post-tropical low and departing high in the Gulf of Maine. \nMax gusts in the 35-50 mph range are expected in the Laurel \nHighlands particularly along and to the west side of the \nhighest ridgetops (downslope wind from the southeast). A Wind \nAdvisory remains in effect for Cambria and Somerset counties. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Warmer and more humid next week with a couple \nrounds of rain showers possible Tuesday and again late in the \nweek\n\nWarmer and noticeably more humid to start next week. Max temps\non Tuesday are forecast to peak 10-15 degrees above the\nhistorical average for mid October with highs in the 70-80F\nrange. Improving weather conditions can be expected Monday as \nthe remnants of Isaias exit the Mid Atlantic. Rain chances on \nTuesday appear to be maximized over the northern tier where a \nshortwave aloft and sfc cold front (infused with moisture from \nTS Rachel?) streak eastward from the Great Lakes. Another period\nof rain is possible later next week, but there is low confidence\nin the timing of the front.",
